# Process environment for the Rust Stado coordinator.
#
# Provider choice and backing-store locators intentionally do not live here.
# Install deploy/azure/stado.config.json at the path below and keep Azure
# primary, S3 backup, managed identity, release channel and agent grant in
# that single deployment config.
STADO_CONFIG=$HOME/.stado/config.json

# Coordinator/dashboard uses consumer stado-control-plane with owner-only grant
# ~/.stado/control-plane-skarbiec-token. Each service path reads only the
# central item/field it needs. Object routes fail closed if
# stado-object-api/token is unreadable; machine routes independently fail
# closed if stado-machine-api/token is unreadable. Neither has an environment
# fallback.

# Azure VMs use the separate stado-azure-agent consumer. Its exact item:read
# allowlist equals agent.skarbiec.items (env override WC_AGENT_SKARBIEC_ITEMS):
# stado-aws plus every item referenced by an allowed workload's secret_env.
# Its owner-only grant is delivered only to the trusted Rust agent through a
# single-read FIFO. Dispatch fails unless the visible item set matches exactly.
# Raw secret values are never placed in cloud-init or submitted-job environments.

# Endpoint contract for consuming deployments. The public base is non-secret;
# trusted-service credentials stay only in the consuming server deployment.
# Publisher/server workloads:
#   STADO_API_URL=https://stado.wisent.com
#   STADO_API_TOKEN=<value from `stado secrets get stado-object-api --field token`>
#   STADO_MACHINE_API_TOKEN=<value from `stado secrets get stado-machine-api --field token`>
# STADO_API_TOKEN authorizes object routes only. STADO_MACHINE_API_TOKEN
# authorizes exactly machine submit/status/cancel; it has no object or Supabase
# fallback. Services needing both object transfer and compute receive separate
# scoped grants for the two exact item/field pairs.
#
# Public browser/read-only workloads use the canonical TLS endpoint:
STADO_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://stado.wisent.com
#
# Public immutable objects use stado://public/<product>/... and resolve only
# through:
#   ${STADO_PUBLIC_BASE_URL}/api/public/object?uri=<urlencoded-stado-uri>
# Never ship STADO_API_TOKEN or STADO_MACHINE_API_TOKEN to browsers. The public
# endpoint exposes no object listing, stat, private namespace, mutation,
# machine operation or trusted-service credential.

# The TLS reverse proxy must forward /api/public/object unchanged to the Rust
# dashboard listener. deploy/azure/Caddyfile.example is the canonical route
# template; it does not add proxy-layer auth to that one path because the Rust
# handler itself restricts it to stado://public/.

# External prerequisites intentionally not provisioned by Stado:
# - Azure storage account and private stado container with the release tree.
# - User-assigned managed identity with Blob Data Contributor and VM
#   self-delete rights, attached through azure.vm_identity_id.
# - Per-region network resources and approved GPU quota.
# - S3 bucket plus stado-aws credentials.
# - Coordinator and restricted agent Skarbiec grants described above.
# - TLS DNS/reverse proxy and a self-hosted control-plane runner.
